in capital  seems to have been located at the spring  of courage everything else about them  suggested tribe who valued power this is  reflected by terms like savage barbarian  and warlike as well as the effects of  the armor itself jackson campbell  proposes the idea that the war paint the  barbarians used for their armor could in  fact be infused with malice the infamous  byproduct of ganon's hatred seen all  across hyrule the reasoning behind this  connection is that some of the stronger  variants of bokoblins moblins lisophos  and linos have similar paint-like  patterns on their body now the game  doesn't outright say that these patterns  are the result of malice but it does  suggest that these types of enemies have  been influenced by ganon's fiendish  magic making them stronger than their  other counterparts and indeed it's hard  to ignore the similarities between the  war paint on the barbarian armor and  those found on ganon's minions  particularly the silver variants  according to jackson this could be yet  another indication that the zonai may  not have been the friendly neighborhood  savages we thought them out to be zeltiq  and i have previously covered the  possibility that the zonai may have had  some sort of allegiance with ganondorf  in the distant past in our collaboration  we pointed to the architecture and cave  painting seen in the sequel footage many  of which feature themes and motifs  related to the demon king also one can't  help but see the similarities in the red  flowing hair on the barbarian helmet and  ganon's iconic red mane from past games  and while this is yet another one of  those possible connections that could  point to some sort of relationship  between the zonai and ganondorf ganon  this supposed alliance could have been  tarnished a long time ago and the  barbarians of farron may not feel the  same way about him now if they're even  alive still i think it's an interesting  idea that the zonai could have taken  inspiration from 